[Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Wednesday October 5th I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal  Elon Musk wanted to buy Twitter then he  didn't now it seems he does again  late on Monday lawyers for musk passed  along a proposal to Twitter seeking to  return to the original terms of the 44  billion dollar deal and on Tuesday  Twitter said it would accept  the agreement could avert a high-stakes  trial that's set to begin October 17th  but musk has been so unpredictable  throughout this Saga so there's no  guarantees on what will happen next  joining us to discuss what's behind this  latest twist and what it could mean for  the future of Twitter is wsj senior  reporter Cara Lombardo hi Cara thanks  for joining me hi there  there's been so much drama in this deal  and now musk and Twitter are back where  they started why is that  well as with everything involving Elon  Musk we don't really know for sure why  the sudden change of heart but people  have a few guesses of course one is that  he was set to be deposed in preparation  for the trial later this week so that  could explain it and what about Twitter  what has Twitter said about Elon musk's  return to his original offer you know  unlike musk they've hardly said a word  this entire time other than that they  intend to close the deal on the original  terms and they've been basically saying  that same line for the past several  months as this is unfolded before we get  into the trial can you just remind us  what those original terms were and how  this all got started yeah so back in  April musk agreed to pay 54.20 a share  to buy Twitter for a total price of  roughly 44 billion dollars and at the  time he was very gung-ho about it he  actually told Twitter he was so  interested he agreed to waive due  diligence or the equivalent of like a  house inspection and wanted to finalize  the deal as soon as possible  that's where they were at the beginning  now they seem to be back where they  started but what was hanging over them  at the moment as you mentioned is this  trial that was going to take place in  just a couple of weeks what did we know  from the early hearings about what the  shape of that trial might be so what  happened was musk had a change of heart  on the deal in beginning in May in July  he  move to terminate the deal saying that  Twitter had misled him about his  business specifically he was concerned  that they underestimated the number of  bots on their platform  and then Twitter sued him to follow  through with the deal as was their right  in their merger agreement the trial was  set to be possibly you know the most  Monumental corporate Clash of all time  this is Elon Musk one of the best known  and most visible billionaire  entrepreneurs out there  facing off against Twitter obviously  ubiquitous social media company that  many of us use in our daily lives so  have any of the issues that he raised as  reasons to pull out of the deal been  addressed as far as We Know  so he raised a variety of issues over  the past several months as this has been  playing out and a lot of legal experts  kind of likened it to throwing things  against the wall to see what sticks he  took issue not only with the Bots but  also with the way Twitter was running  its business in the meantime before he  took control of it that he didn't like  that they had let some Executives go  without seeking his permission first so  he was trying a variety of things none  of which legal experts thought were  particularly strong arguments  as the case moved forward we've seen  musk add claims to the counter suit  including he Incorporated complaints  from the former head of security at  Twitter who came forward as a  whistleblower in August alleging  problems with the platform's data  security did it seem like those would  affect his chances of getting out of  this deal until recently we were  thinking maybe that's the one wild card  should he be able to find a Smoking Gun  courtesy of this whistleblower maybe  that changes the direction of the case  but so far we hadn't seen anything in  the whistleblowers report nor in what  has come out through Discovery so far in  the trial that appeared poised to change  the outcome so what are the next steps  that have to happen here well I think  the way it's it's playing out is Twitter  would like obviously some comfort that  musk will follow through with this  proposal to close the deal on the  original term so they've gotten the  Delaware judge who was to oversee the  trial involved their ha the two sides  are hashing out a potential plan to get  get the deal closed and assuming the  judge signs off on that plan they would  be well on their way to doing so  given that must  character what  what maybe expect to happen next so yeah  I mean that's the thing in a normal  scenario when two sides say hey do you  want to do this yes we probably want to  do that you expect now they can agree  and close the deal pretty quickly here  but it's musk and everything he's done  on this deal has been completely against  Norms so you know we have to wonder will  he actually follow through but hopefully  we'll get an answer pretty soon if they  do reach an agreement we expect the deal  could close as soon as within a week  all right that's our reporter Carol  Lombardo thanks for breaking this down  for us Cara thanks  and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing if you want more Tech stories  check out our website wsj.com I'm Zoe  Thomas for The Wall Street Journal  thanks for listening  [Music]

